<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         AQUILA DARK — the default theme.

         A theme dresses the WINDOW: background, cards, borders, accent. What the data is painted with
         comes from the active preset, and the two are chosen independently. Nothing in here mentions a
         sensor.

         It is merged on top of WPF-UI's Fluent dark base, so it only has to name the keys it disagrees
         with; everything unlisted stays Fluent. That keeps a theme small enough to read in one sitting and
         to survive a WPF-UI upgrade.

         The surfaces are cool neutrals, barely off grey. The warmth is all in the accent and in whatever
         preset is painting the data — the ground is the one thing on screen that is not saying anything,
         so it stays out of the way of everything that is.

         ── The file has two halves, and the split is the point ──

         THE PALETTE names LEVELS, not parts. A level is a depth, and several things legitimately live at
         the same one: a card, a title-bar chip, a StatBox and the stacked bar's track are all "Card". They
         say so here, once, so they cannot drift apart — which they did, for months, until four of them were
         found sharing a colour by accident and a fifth had wandered off.

         Naming by part instead — Chip.Fill, StatBox.Fill, Track.Fill — gives three entries that are equal
         today and need not be tomorrow. Naming by level makes the sharing deliberate.

         THE ASSIGNMENTS say who lives where. Every key below, ours or the kit's, points at a level and
         states nothing of its own. To restyle, move a level; to move one part between levels, change one
         line here.

         The level names are the app's own and the same in every theme, so there is one language for depth
         rather than one per file.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- ── THE PALETTE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Darkest to lightest. Each comment says who lives at that level. -->

    <!-- Under the window: the recessed ground behind scrolling content. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Below">#1A1A1E</Color>

    <!-- The window itself. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.App">#202024</Color>

    <!-- Raised: cards, the title-bar chips, StatBox, HeaderChip, the stacked bar's track, the storage
         rows. Anything small that sits ON the page rather than being the page. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Card">#2A2A2F</Color>

    <!-- Something you can press or type in, at rest. From the design canvas, and solid: the kit's
         translucent fills wash out over Mica, which is why buttons read as glyphs floating beside a title
         instead of as things you can press. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Control">#33333A</Color>

    <!-- The same control under the pointer, and pressed. Lighter then darker, which is the direction
         Fluent moves in. These are STATES: nothing at rest may borrow them — that is precisely how tuning
         a hover once repainted seven resting surfaces across the app. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Hover">#3C3C44</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Pressed">#2B2B31</Color>

    <!-- A control that cannot be used. BELOW the resting face, which is the whole point: the kit's own
         disabled state is a translucency — white at about four percent over whatever is behind — and over
         Mica it came out at #353435, LIGHTER than a control you can actually press. A dead control glowing
         more brightly than a live one is the statement backwards.

         Solid, and a level like any other, so it recedes by the same amount everywhere regardless of the
         wallpaper underneath.

         THE FACE of every disabled control, whatever the control. With Aquila.Line.Disabled and
         Aquila.Text.Disabled below, these three are the whole of what "unavailable" looks like in this
         application — one line each, and no control may state it for itself. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Disabled">#26262B</Color>

    <!-- Scrim over the window's Mica: App at ~70%. Our colour leads and the material is left as depth
         rather than as the thing you see — the palette is drawn from the logo, and on Windows' pale grey
         material the ambers read as ordinary orange instead of as flame.

         Written out rather than derived, because XAML cannot add an alpha to another colour. Keep the last
         six digits equal to App. Tinting the material itself is not possible: WPF-UI drives the modern DWM
         backdrop, which takes a type and no colour, and opacity on the brush would fade every control
         above it too. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Surface.Scrim">#B3202024</Color>

    <!-- The outline of anything you can press or type in. One colour, and every name for it points here:
         a field is built from TWO stacked borders, the second of which draws the bottom edge alone —
         Fluent's elevation line — from a key of its own. Set one and not the other and three sides change
         while the fourth does not, which reads as a missing bottom border rather than as two colours.

         SOLID, not a white at a fifth. A translucent line takes its real colour from whatever is behind it,
         and behind this is Mica — which changes with the wallpaper. The outline was drifting from window to
         window and from desktop to desktop; stated outright it is the same line everywhere. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Line">#48484E</Color>

    <!-- The same outline, asserted: a control under the pointer. Solid too, and a step up from the line
         above — leaving a translucent white beside a solid grey is how a family stops being one. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Line.Strong">#5C5C62</Color>

    <!-- Just off white: pure white on the control face is the hardest thing on screen and pulls the eye to
         the chrome instead of the data. -->
    <!-- The outline of a control that cannot be used. A step below Aquila.Line, and it happens to equal
         the resting control face — which is exactly why it needs a name of its own rather than pointing at
         Aquila.Surface.Control. Sharing a value is fine; sharing an ENTRY means changing the disabled
         outline would repaint the face of every live control on the way past. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Line.Disabled">#33333A</Color>

    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Text">#D6D6D8</Color>

    <!-- The same text, unavailable. Far enough down to read as off rather than as merely dim. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Text.Disabled">#6E6E74</Color>

    <!-- The shell's own highlight — rail, focus, buttons — and never a reading. WPF-UI derives its whole
         accent family from this one value.

         Sharing a hue with step 4 of a data ramp is fine, and only here: colour means intensity on the
         data and nothing on the chrome. A deeper ember than the amber this once was; #F5A623 is where the
         logo's gradient starts and read as pale for chrome.

         THE ONLY STATEMENT OF IT. AppearanceService reads this key by name and hands it to WPF-UI, which
         derives its whole accent family from it — the primary buttons, the focus outline, the navigation
         rail. So this one line moves every orange in the application, and nothing else needs touching. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Accent">#FF6B35</Color>

    <!-- Two shades OF the accent, rather than two colours. XAML has no arithmetic — it cannot put an alpha
         on a colour or lighten one — so they have to be written out. They are written HERE, touching the
         accent, so that keeping them in step is a matter of reading three adjacent lines instead of
         remembering that they exist somewhere else.

         The wash is the accent at about twelve percent, for a control that is CHOSEN: an outline over a
         tint of itself, never a solid block — a filled state button competes with the data it is about,
         and there are four of them in a row. The quiet one is the accent lifted towards white, so the
         chosen item reads warm rather than merely brighter. -->
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Accent.Wash">#1FFF6B35</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Accent.WashOver">#33FF6B35</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Aquila.Accent.Quiet">#FFB597</Color>

    <!-- ── THE ASSIGNMENTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
         Who lives at which level. Nothing below states a colour of its own. -->

    <!-- Optional: leave it out and the badges and dashed "add" outlines take the accent the kit is using,
         which here is Aquila.Accent anyway. Declared so this is visibly the place to give them a colour of
         their own. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Chrome.Accent"                   Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Aquila.Window.Scrim"                    Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Scrim}"/>

    <!-- Surfaces -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ApplicationBackgroundBrush"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.App}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush"      Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.App}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorSecondaryBrush" Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Below}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LayerFillColorDefaultBrush"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Card}"/>

    <!-- There is no WELL. A level for the inside of an open CardExpander was declared here and painted
         nothing: the kit binds the expanded content's Border to CardBackground, the same key as the
         header, so there is no second colour to give it without retemplating the control.

         Recorded rather than quietly dropped, because the idea is sound and may come back — it just needs
         a template before it needs a colour. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush"    Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Card}"/>

    <!-- Controls -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlFillColorDefaultBrush"           Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlFillColorSecondaryBrush"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlFillColorTertiaryBrush"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Pressed}"/>

    <!-- Lines. The three kit keys below are the two borders a field is built from, plus the outline every
         other control wears — all one colour on purpose. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlElevationBorderBrush"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlElevationBorderBrush"        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlStrokeColorSecondaryBrush"       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Strong}"/>

    <!-- A card has NO EDGE. It is told apart from the ground by being lighter than it, and a line around
         that was saying the same thing a second time — four cards in a column read as a list of outlines
         rather than as four things. The border is kept at one pixel and painted the colour of the face, so
         the card is simply one pixel bigger; transparent would instead let the ground show through as a
         hairline halo, which is a line again.

         CardBorderBrush and CardBackground, not CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush and CardBackgroundFillColor-
         DefaultBrush: the card is another kit control with a family of its own, found by building one and
         asking its Border which resource it was holding. The two stroke keys were painted magenta and cyan
         to see where they land and neither appeared anywhere in the app, so they are not claimed — a key
         claimed for something that does not exist is a claim nobody can check. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackground"                         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Card}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBorderBrush"                        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Card}"/>
    <!-- The one state a card says anything in. Neither kit template has a hover trigger, so these keys
         are reached only through the IsMouseOver rule in Controls.xaml — the rule says when, these say
         what. CardBorderBrushPointerOver is OURS: WPF-UI has no such key, so do not go looking for it in
         the kit. It exists because the face and the edge have to move together, and the edge has no
         hover key of its own to move with.

         Both are the same level, and it is the level that makes the card grow rather than gain an outline:
         an edge that stayed at the resting colour would appear as a dark ring the moment you pointed at
         it — an outline arriving exactly when we had decided a card has none.

         CardBackgroundPressed and CardBorderBrushPressed were here and are gone: neither card has an
         IsPressed trigger in its template or its style chain, so nothing ever read them.

         It borrows the resting CONTROL face rather than earning a level of its own. The two are the same
         thing said twice over: "the surface directly under your pointer". A level shared on purpose is
         what the palette is for; a fifth grey that happens to equal the fourth is what it exists to
         prevent. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundPointerOver"              Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBorderBrushPointerOver"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>

    <!-- ── Controls that keep a family of their own ──────────────────
         A field, a combo and a switch each carry a full set of keys under their own name, and read NONE of
         the ControlFill ones above. Setting Background on the control does nothing at all: the template
         never looks at it. This is the same shape of problem as the elevation line — a second name for one
         thing, and only one of the two names being the one that paints.

         So every such family is brought back onto the palette here, one level each, rather than being left
         at the kit's value beside controls that already follow ours. The test is a number box next to a
         button: if they are not the same grey, a family has been missed.

         Focused is deliberately the SAME level as at rest. Focus is already said by the accent underline;
         saying it a second time with the face makes the field jump as the caret lands in it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackground"                  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundPointerOver"       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundFocused"           Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundDisabled"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushDisabled"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForegroundDisabled"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackground"                     Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundPointerOver"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundFocused"              Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundDisabled"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- The open list. It wears the CONTROL face rather than a plane of its own, because that is what it
         is: the combo you just opened, continued downwards. A card's level would have made a list dropped
         over a card invisibly the same colour as it.

         ComboBoxItemBackgroundSelected is the row under the POINTER, whatever the name says — read out of
         the template, the trigger is IsHighlighted, which is one state for the pointer and the keyboard
         together. There is no separate hover key to give it, so the row takes the hover level and the
         two inputs agree by construction.

         What says "this is the chosen one" is the accent pill down the left edge, which is the kit's and
         stays the kit's. The text is not asked to say it a second time. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownBackground"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownBorderBrush"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownGlyphForeground"        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemBackgroundSelected"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemForeground"                 Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxItemForegroundSelected"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>

    <!-- The scroll bar, four keys and all four live — read off a real one rather than guessed.

         ScrollBarTrackFillPointerOver is THE TRACK, not a hover state: the template binds the track's
         Border to it outright and lets the expand animation decide when the track is seen at all. Third
         key today whose name describes a state it does not have.

         The track and the end buttons take the control face, because an expanded scroll bar IS a control
         you are about to drag. The thumb takes the assertive line: it is the one part that has to be
         found against both the track when expanded AND the page when collapsed, and a line level is what
         we already use for "visible without being loud".

         The arrows take the text colour. They are glyphs, and a glyph too quiet to read is a button with
         no label. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarTrackFillPointerOver"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarButtonBackground"              Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarThumbFill"                     Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Strong}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrollBarButtonArrowForeground"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>

    <!-- The slider. Its GEOMETRY needed nothing: the kit already builds a four pixel track with a radius
         of two and a twelve pixel thumb dot, which is exactly what the design canvas asks for, so there is
         no rule for this control at all — only colour.

         Built from three painted parts, read off a live one: a track, an outer circle and a dot inside it.
         Both halves of the thumb take the TEXT level, so it comes out as one plain pale disc.

         The dot was the accent, and it is deliberately not. The accent is how this application says
         something about itself — a primary button, the chosen one of four, a rail. A slider's handle is
         not making a statement; it is showing where a number currently sits, and a number is not an
         opinion. Orange there also put a second accent on screen beside the widget the slider is editing,
         where the only orange should be the data's. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackFill"                        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackFillPointerOver"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Strong}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTickBarFill"                      Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderOuterThumbBackground"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderThumbBackground"                  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderThumbBackgroundPointerOver"       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>

    <!-- The thumb's edge. A gradient of translucent black in the kit, which is the same shape of problem
         as the field's elevation line: a translucency takes its real colour from whatever it lands on.
         Solid, and the same line as everything else round here. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CircleElevationBorderBrush"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxBorderBrushDisabled"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ComboBoxForegroundDisabled"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- The button has one too. Its resting face was the only one of these that already looked right, which
         means the kit happens to point it at the same place we do — happens to, with nothing holding it
         there. Said outright it stops being a coincidence.

         The FOREGROUND is deliberately not claimed for the live states: an accent button is white-on-orange
         and takes that from here, so naming a text colour would grey the lettering on every primary button
         in the app. Only the disabled one is ours, where there is no accent to disagree with. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackground"                       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPointerOver"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPressed"                Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Pressed}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundDisabled"               Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBorderBrushDisabled"              Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonForegroundDisabled"               Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- A button that stays down. Checked is left to the kit's accent, like everything else chosen. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackground"                 Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundPointerOver"      Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundPressed"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Pressed}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundDisabled"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBorderBrushDisabled"        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonForegroundDisabled"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- The generic pair, for everything with no family of its own: check box, list, the number box's
         chevrons. Last line of defence rather than the main mechanism. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlFillColorDisabledBrush"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextFillColorDisabledBrush"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- The rest of "unavailable", so that nothing is left at the kit's translucency. A check box; a card
         you cannot open; a toggle button that is down AND disabled; an accent button that is disabled,
         which has to stop being orange or it reads as the one thing on screen asking to be pressed.

         Left alone deliberately: SubtleFillColorDisabled, because a subtle control is TRANSPARENT at rest
         and a solid face would appear from nowhere the moment it was disabled. RadioButton, Hyperlink and
         TimePicker keys are not here because the app has none of those — a key claimed for a control that
         does not exist is a claim nobody can check. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundFillUncheckedDisabled"   Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxCheckBackgroundStrokeUncheckedDisabled" Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CheckBoxForegroundUncheckedDisabled"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundDisabled"                 Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBorderBrushDisabled"                Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardForegroundDisabled"                 Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- A toggle button that is DOWN. The kit paints it as a solid accent block with near-black lettering,
         which is right for Fluent and wrong here: our chosen state is an outline over a wash of itself, so
         the kit's text colour — chosen to sit on solid orange — landed black on a dark ground and the word
         disappeared. Four of these sit in a row under "Preview on desktop"; the selected one was the only
         one that could not be read.

         ToggleButtonForegroundCheckedPointerOver IS THE HOVER FILL, not a text colour. Read straight out
         of the template: every other state pairs Panel.Background with a ...Background... key, and this
         one alone reaches for the ...Foreground... one. It is a mis-wiring in WPF-UI 4.3.0, so the name
         cannot be trusted and the value has to suit what it actually paints. Nothing sets the lettering in
         that state, which is why it keeps whatever ToggleButtonForegroundChecked gave it.

         The checked OUTLINE is the kit's AccentControlElevationBorderBrush and is not ours to place: it
         already holds our accent, because AppearanceService fills the whole accent family from it. A
         BorderBrush setter was written in the Style for this and never did anything — the template sets
         Border.BorderBrush by name, which beats a style setter on the control. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundChecked"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent.Wash}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonForegroundCheckedPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent.WashOver}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundCheckedPressed"   Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent.WashOver}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonForegroundChecked"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent.Quiet}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonForegroundCheckedPressed"   Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent.Quiet}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBorderBrushCheckedPressed"  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Accent}"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundCheckedDisabled"  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBorderBrushCheckedDisabled" Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonForegroundCheckedDisabled"  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- A disabled accent button INHERITS the general disabled look rather than keeping a dimmed accent of
         its own — face, outline and text, all three. It is the same argument as everywhere else: there is
         one thing "unavailable" looks like, and a control does not get to say it differently. The outline
         is the one that is easy to forget, and forgetting it leaves a button wearing our face and our text
         with the kit's edge — the third thing that should not exist. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentFillColorDisabledBrush"           Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlStrokeColorOnAccentDisabledBrush" Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTextFillColorDisabledBrush"       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnAccentFillColorDisabledBrush"     Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- Only the switch's OFF side is claimed. On stays the kit's accent: a switch that is on is a thing
         that is chosen, and everything chosen in this app answers to the accent. The knob is left alone
         too — it is the moving part, and it reads against whichever side it is sitting on. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOff"                    Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Control}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOffPointerOver"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Hover}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOffPressed"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Pressed}"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOff"                  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOffPointerOver"       Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Strong}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOffPressed"           Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Strong}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOffDisabled"            Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOffDisabled"          Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchFillOnDisabled"             Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Surface.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchStrokeOnDisabled"           Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Line.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchKnobFillOffDisabled"        Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchKnobFillOnDisabled"         Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleSwitchContentForegroundDisabled"  Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text.Disabled}"/>

    <!-- Text -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextFillColorPrimaryBrush"              Color="{StaticResource Aquila.Text}"/>

    <!-- ── EXCEPTIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
         A control that genuinely needs a colour of its own, rather than a level, belongs here — declared
         as an exception so it reads as a decision instead of an oversight. Empty is the healthy state. -->

</ResourceDictionary>
